 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "# 1.03 — Rotations of Space and Quaternions\n\nThe argument of 1.02 never mentioned the dimension. Polarisation, orthonormal bases and the rest work verbatim in $\\mathbb R^3$, so once again every isometry is $x \\mapsto Ax + b$ with $A^{\\mathsf T}A = I$, and $\\det A = \\pm 1$ splits $O(3)$ into two halves.\n\nWhat changes is the structure of the rotation half, $SO(3)$, and it changes a great deal:\n\n- **$SO(3)$ is non-abelian.** In the plane, rotations commute because there is only one axis available. In space there are infinitely many, and the order of two rotations matters — a fact you can verify with a book and two turns.\n- **Every rotation still has an axis.** This is *Euler's theorem*, and it is not obvious. In odd dimensions a rotation must fix a line; in even dimensions (the plane included) it need not.\n- **$SO(3)$ is doubly covered by the unit quaternions.** There is a two-to-one map from the unit sphere $S^3$ onto the rotation group. This is a genuinely topological fact with practical consequences, and it is why quaternions beat Euler angles for representing orientation.\n\nThis notebook works through all three, and takes one step past them: writing a rotation as $\\exp(\\theta K)$ for a skew-symmetric $K$. That exponential is our first sight of a **Lie algebra**, the object Stage 5 is built on.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## 1. Euler's theorem: every rotation has an axis\n\n> **Theorem (Euler).** Every $A \\in SO(3)$ has $+1$ as an eigenvalue. The corresponding eigenvector is the **axis**, and $A$ is a rotation about it.\n\nThe proof is a determinant trick, and it is short enough to give in full:\n\n$$\\det(A - I) = \\det(A - A A^{\\mathsf T}) = \\det A \\cdot \\det(I - A^{\\mathsf T}) = \\det(I - A^{\\mathsf T}) = \\det(I - A),$$\n\nusing $\\det A = 1$ and $\\det M = \\det M^{\\mathsf T}$. But for a $3\\times3$ matrix $\\det(I - A) = (-1)^3\\det(A - I) = -\\det(A-I)$. So $\\det(A - I) = -\\det(A - I)$, forcing $\\det(A - I) = 0$ — and that is exactly the statement that $1$ is an eigenvalue.\n\n**Where the dimension enters.** The step $(-1)^n = -1$ needs $n$ odd. In the plane, $(-1)^2 = +1$ and the argument collapses — correctly so, since a plane rotation through $\\theta \\ne 0$ fixes no direction at all. Euler's theorem is a fact about odd dimensions, not about rotations in general.\n\nSince $A$ is orthogonal it preserves the plane perpendicular to the axis, and acts there as a plane rotation. So the eigenvalues are $1, e^{i\\theta}, e^{-i\\theta}$, and the trace gives us $\\theta$ for free:\n\n$$\\operatorname{tr} A = 1 + 2\\cos\\theta .$$",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"ax-plots\")$\nratprint : false$\n\n/* Helpers.  Note cvec, not col — col is a Maxima builtin used by invert(). */\ncvec(v)  := transpose(matrix(v))$\nvnorm(a) := sqrt(a . a)$\ncross(a, b) := [ a[2]*b[3] - a[3]*b[2],\n                 a[3]*b[1] - a[1]*b[3],\n                 a[1]*b[2] - a[2]*b[1] ]$\ntsimp(e) := if matrixp(e) then matrixmap(lambda([z], trigrat(z)), e)\n            else if listp(e) then map(lambda([z], trigrat(z)), e)\n            else trigrat(e)$\n\n/* Elementary rotations about the three coordinate axes */\nRx(θ) := matrix([1, 0, 0], [0, cos(θ), -sin(θ)], [0, sin(θ), cos(θ)])$\nRy(θ) := matrix([cos(θ), 0, sin(θ)], [0, 1, 0], [-sin(θ), 0, cos(θ)])$\nRz(θ) := matrix([cos(θ), -sin(θ), 0], [sin(θ), cos(θ), 0], [0, 0, 1])$\n\n/* A \"random\" rotation, built by composing three of them */\nAtest : tsimp( Rz(π/3) . Ry(π/4) . Rx(π/6) )$\nprint(\"A = \", Atest)$\nprint(\"AᵀA = \", tsimp(transpose(Atest) . Atest))$\nprint(\"det A = \", tsimp(determinant(Atest)))$\n\n/* Euler's theorem: det(A − I) vanishes identically for A ∈ SO(3) */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"det(A − I) = \", tsimp(determinant(Atest - ident(3))), \"  (Euler)\")$\nprint(\"eigenvalues of A: \", float(eigenvalues(Atest)[1]))$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 1,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "A =\n    matrix([1/2^(3/2),(sqrt(2)-6)/8,((sqrt(2)+2)*sqrt(3))/8],\n           [sqrt(3)/2^(3/2),((sqrt(2)+2)*sqrt(3))/8,(3*sqrt(2)-2)/8],\n           [-(1/sqrt(2)),1/2^(3/2),sqrt(3)/2^(3/2)])\nAᵀA =  matrix([1,0,0],[0,1,0],[0,0,1])\ndet A =  1\ndet(A − I) =  0   (Euler)\neigenvalues of A:\n                  [-(0.0078125*(119.78090450081223*%i-45.127983745867866)),\n                   0.0078125*(119.78090450081223*%i+45.127983745867866),1.0]"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "### Recovering the axis and angle\n\nGiven $A$, both the axis and the angle can be read straight off, with no eigenvector computation.\n\nThe **angle** comes from the trace, as noted: $\\cos\\theta = (\\operatorname{tr}A - 1)/2$.\n\nThe **axis** comes from the antisymmetric part. Split $A = S + K$ into symmetric and antisymmetric halves, $K = \\tfrac12(A - A^{\\mathsf T})$. A $3\\times3$ antisymmetric matrix has only three independent entries, so it can be identified with a vector — the operation\n\n$$K = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & -k_3 & k_2 \\\\ k_3 & 0 & -k_1 \\\\ -k_2 & k_1 & 0\\end{pmatrix} \\;\\longleftrightarrow\\; k = (k_1, k_2, k_3),$$\n\nwith the defining property $Kv = k \\times v$ for every $v$. Applying this to $\\tfrac12(A - A^{\\mathsf T})$ gives a vector along the axis, of length $\\sin\\theta$:\n\n$$(a_{32} - a_{23},\\; a_{13} - a_{31},\\; a_{21} - a_{12}) = 2\\sin\\theta \\cdot \\hat n .$$\n\nThis identification of antisymmetric matrices with vectors is another three-dimensional coincidence, of exactly the same kind as the cross product in 1.01 — and it is no accident, since $Kv = k \\times v$ is literally the cross product. The general statement, which Stage 5 needs, is that the antisymmetric matrices form the **Lie algebra** $\\mathfrak{so}(3)$, and in dimension 3 it happens to be three-dimensional.\n\nNote the failure cases: at $\\theta = 0$ there is no axis to find (everything is fixed), and at $\\theta = \\pi$ we get $\\sin\\theta = 0$ and the formula degenerates — $A$ is then symmetric and the antisymmetric part vanishes entirely.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "load(\"linearalgebra\")$   /* provides mat_trace */\n\n/* The hat map: vector → antisymmetric matrix, with  hat(k)·v = k × v  */\nhat(k) := matrix([  0,   -k[3],  k[2]],\n                 [ k[3],   0,   -k[1]],\n                 [-k[2],  k[1],   0  ])$\n\n/* The inverse \"vee\" map, applied to the antisymmetric part of A */\nvee(M) := [ M[3][2] - M[2][3], M[1][3] - M[3][1], M[2][1] - M[1][2] ]$\n\n/* Check hat(k)v = k × v symbolically */\nkk : [k1, k2, k3]$  vv : [v1, v2, v3]$\nprint(\"hat(k)v − k×v = \", ratsimp( args(transpose(hat(kk) . cvec(vv)))[1] - cross(kk, vv) ))$\n\n/* Extract axis and angle from a rotation matrix */\naxis_angle(A) := block([ct, st, w, θ],\n  ct : tsimp((mat_trace(A) - 1)/2),\n  w  : tsimp(vee(A)),\n  st : tsimp(vnorm(w)/2),\n  θ  : atan2(st, ct),\n  [ tsimp(w / (2*st)), θ ] )$\n\naa : axis_angle(Atest)$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"axis  = \", float(aa[1]))$\nprint(\"angle = \", float(aa[2]), \" rad = \", float(aa[2]*180/π), \"°\")$\n\n/* Verify: the axis is genuinely fixed, and the trace formula holds */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"A·axis − axis = \", float( tsimp(Atest . cvec(aa[1])) - cvec(float(aa[1])) ))$\nprint(\"tr A = \", float(mat_trace(Atest)), \"   1 + 2cos θ = \", float(1 + 2*cos(aa[2])))$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 3,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "hat(k)v − k×v =  [0,0,0]\naxis  =  [0.039123861357911634,0.7727739679798359,0.6334743229880322]\nangle =  1.210488433409354  rad =  69.35587838375878 °\nA·axis − axis =  matrix([0.0],[0.0],[0.0])\ntr A =  1.7051247460291845    1 + 2cos θ =  1.7051247460291852"
     ]
    }
   ]
