  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## 2. Rodrigues' formula, and rotation as an exponential\n\nGoing the other way — from an axis $\\hat n$ and angle $\\theta$ to the matrix — gives **Rodrigues' rotation formula**. With $K = \\widehat{n}$ (so $Kv = \\hat n \\times v$):\n\n$$R(\\hat n, \\theta) \\;=\\; I + \\sin\\theta\\, K + (1 - \\cos\\theta)\\,K^2 .$$\n\nThe derivation is a decomposition of exactly the kind we used throughout 1.01. Split $v$ into its component along the axis, which the rotation fixes, and its perpendicular component, which rotates in the plane:\n\n$$v = (\\hat n \\cdot v)\\hat n + v_\\perp, \\qquad R v = (\\hat n \\cdot v)\\hat n + \\cos\\theta\\, v_\\perp + \\sin\\theta\\,(\\hat n \\times v).$$\n\nRewriting $v_\\perp = -K^2 v$ and $\\hat n \\times v = Kv$ gives the formula.\n\n### The exponential\n\nRodrigues' formula is really a disguised exponential. The key algebraic fact is\n\n$$K^3 = -K,$$\n\nwhich makes every power of $K$ reduce to $K$ or $K^2$. Feeding that into the exponential series and collecting terms:\n\n$$e^{\\theta K} = I + \\left(\\theta - \\frac{\\theta^3}{3!} + \\cdots\\right)K + \\left(\\frac{\\theta^2}{2!} - \\frac{\\theta^4}{4!} + \\cdots\\right)K^2 = I + \\sin\\theta\\,K + (1-\\cos\\theta)K^2 .$$\n\nSo $R(\\hat n, \\theta) = \\exp(\\theta \\widehat n)$, and **every rotation is the exponential of an antisymmetric matrix**.\n\nThis is the single most important sentence in the notebook. The antisymmetric matrices form a vector space $\\mathfrak{so}(3)$ — flat, closed under addition, easy — while the rotations form a curved group $SO(3)$. The exponential maps one onto the other. Stage 5.02 develops this for every matrix group; here we can watch it work in coordinates.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* Rodrigues' formula for a unit axis n and angle θ */\nRodrigues(n, θ) := block([K],\n  K : hat(n),\n  tsimp( ident(3) + sin(θ)*K + (1 - cos(θ))*(K . K) ) )$\n\n/* The algebraic fact that makes it an exponential: K³ = −K for a unit axis.\n   ratsubst rewrites n3² → 1 − n1² − n2² even inside higher powers. */\nnsym : [n1, n2, n3]$\nKsym : hat(nsym)$\nunit(e) := ratsimp( ratsubst(1 - n1^2 - n2^2, n3^2, expand(e)) )$\n\nprint(\"K³ + K  (using |n| = 1) = \")$\nprint( matrixmap(unit, Ksym . Ksym . Ksym + Ksym) )$\n\n/* K² = nnᵀ − I, the negative of the projection onto the plane ⊥ n */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"K² − (nnᵀ − I) = \")$\nprint( matrixmap(unit, Ksym . Ksym - (cvec(nsym) . transpose(cvec(nsym)) - ident(3))) )$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 5,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "K³ + K  (using |n| = 1) =\nmatrix([0,0,0],[0,0,0],[0,0,0])\nK² − (nnᵀ − I) =\nmatrix([0,0,0],[0,0,0],[0,0,0])"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* A concrete rotation: 120° about the diagonal (1,1,1)/√3.\n   This should cyclically permute the coordinate axes. */\nndiag : [1,1,1]/sqrt(3)$\nR120  : Rodrigues(ndiag, 2*π/3)$\nprint(\"R(120° about (1,1,1)) = \", R120)$\nprint(\"  e1 ↦ \", transpose(tsimp(R120 . cvec([1,0,0]))))$\nprint(\"  e2 ↦ \", transpose(tsimp(R120 . cvec([0,1,0]))))$\nprint(\"  e3 ↦ \", transpose(tsimp(R120 . cvec([0,0,1]))), \"  — a 3-cycle\")$\nprint(\"  R³ = \", tsimp(R120 . R120 . R120), \"  (order 3)\")$\n\n/* Rodrigues really is the exponential: sum the series numerically and compare */\nexpseries(M, N) := block([S, T],\n  S : ident(3), T : ident(3),\n  for j : 1 thru N do ( T : (T . M)/j, S : S + T ),\n  S )$\n\nnax : [0, 3/5, 4/5]$          /* a unit axis */\nθ0  : 0.7$\nRex : float( expseries(θ0 * hat(nax), 20) )$\nRrd : float( Rodrigues(nax, θ0) )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"‖exp(θK) − Rodrigues‖ = \",\n      float( sqrt( sum( sum( (Rex[i][j] - Rrd[i][j])^2, j, 1, 3), i, 1, 3) ) ))$\n\n/* Round trip: matrix → (axis, angle) → matrix */\nback : axis_angle(Rodrigues(nax, θ0))$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"recovered axis  = \", float(back[1]), \"   original = \", float(nax))$\nprint(\"recovered angle = \", float(back[2]), \"   original = \", θ0)$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 6,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "R(120° about (1,1,1)) =  matrix([0,0,1],[1,0,0],[0,1,0])\n  e1 ↦  matrix([0,1,0])\n  e2 ↦  matrix([0,0,1])\n  e3 ↦  matrix([1,0,0])   — a 3-cycle\n  R³ =  matrix([1,0,0],[0,1,0],[0,0,1])   (order 3)\n‖exp(θK) − Rodrigues‖ =  1.7164753505144179e-15\nrecovered axis  =  [0.0,0.5999999999999992,0.8000000000000007]    original =\n                  [0.0,0.6,0.8]\nrecovered angle =  0.7000000000000005    original =  0.7"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## 3. Non-commutativity, Euler angles, and gimbal lock\n\n$SO(2)$ was abelian; $SO(3)$ is emphatically not. Rotating $90°$ about $x$ then $90°$ about $y$ does not give the same result as doing them in the other order, and no amount of care with conventions will make it so.\n\n**Euler angles** parametrise a rotation by three successive turns about coordinate axes, e.g. $R = R_z(\\phi)R_y(\\theta)R_x(\\psi)$. Three numbers for a three-dimensional group — the count is right, and the parametrisation is nearly always invertible. But \"nearly\" is doing a lot of work.\n\nAt $\\theta = \\pm\\pi/2$ the middle rotation aligns the first and third axes, and the two outer rotations become indistinguishable: only their sum (or difference) has any effect. A degree of freedom is lost, and the parametrisation degenerates. This is **gimbal lock**, and it is a genuine defect of the coordinate chart rather than anything happening in $SO(3)$ itself.\n\nThe deeper reason is topological, and worth stating even though the tools come later: $SO(3)$ is **not homeomorphic to a product of three circles**. So no three-angle parametrisation can be globally non-degenerate — any such chart must fail somewhere, exactly as no single chart covers a sphere (a fact Stage 3.03 and topology-journey Stage 3.01 both make precise). Gimbal lock is not a bug to be engineered away; it is a shadow of the shape of the group.\n\nBelow we watch both facts happen: non-commutativity, and the collapse of the Euler parametrisation.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* --- Non-commutativity, concretely --- */\nP : tsimp( Rx(π/2) . Ry(π/2) )$\nQ : tsimp( Ry(π/2) . Rx(π/2) )$\nprint(\"Rx(90°)Ry(90°) = \", P)$\nprint(\"Ry(90°)Rx(90°) = \", Q)$\nprint(\"they differ:     \", tsimp(P - Q))$\nprint(\"axis/angle of the first : \", float(axis_angle(P)))$\nprint(\"axis/angle of the second: \", float(axis_angle(Q)))$\n\n/* --- Euler angles (Z-Y-X) and gimbal lock --- */\nEuler(φ, θ, ψ) := tsimp( Rz(φ) . Ry(θ) . Rx(ψ) )$\n\n/* Away from the singularity the three angles act independently */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"generic Euler matrix, entry (3,1) = \", Euler(φ, θ, ψ)[3][1], \"  (= −sin θ)\")$\n\n/* At θ = π/2 the matrix depends on φ and ψ only through φ − ψ */\nG : tsimp( Euler(φ, π/2, ψ) )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"at θ = π/2:\")$\nprint(G)$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"same matrix with φ → φ+c, ψ → ψ+c  (c arbitrary):\")$\nprint(\"difference = \", tsimp( subst([φ = φ + c, ψ = ψ + c], G) - G ))$\nprint(\"— one whole degree of freedom has vanished: gimbal lock\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 7,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
