  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## 4. Quaternions and the double cover\n\nHamilton's quaternions are $\\mathbb H = \\{a + bi + cj + dk\\}$ with\n\n$$i^2 = j^2 = k^2 = ijk = -1,$$\n\nfrom which $ij = k$, $ji = -k$ and the rest follow. Multiplication is associative but **not** commutative — which is exactly what we need, since $SO(3)$ is not commutative either.\n\nWrite $q = (s, \\mathbf v)$ with scalar part $s$ and vector part $\\mathbf v$. The product is\n\n$$(s_1, \\mathbf v_1)(s_2, \\mathbf v_2) = \\bigl(s_1 s_2 - \\mathbf v_1 \\cdot \\mathbf v_2,\\; s_1\\mathbf v_2 + s_2\\mathbf v_1 + \\mathbf v_1 \\times \\mathbf v_2\\bigr),$$\n\nso both products from 1.01 appear inside a single quaternion multiplication — the dot product in the scalar slot, the cross product in the vector slot. That is a fair summary of why quaternions work in three dimensions and nothing similar works in four.\n\n### Rotation by conjugation\n\nFor a **unit** quaternion $q$ and a vector $v$ regarded as $(0, v)$, the map\n\n$$v \\;\\longmapsto\\; q\\,v\\,\\bar q, \\qquad \\bar q = (s, -\\mathbf v)$$\n\nis a rotation. Specifically, the unit quaternion\n\n$$q = \\left(\\cos\\tfrac\\theta2,\\; \\sin\\tfrac\\theta2\\,\\hat n\\right)$$\n\nproduces rotation by $\\theta$ about $\\hat n$.\n\n### The half-angle, and why the cover is double\n\nNotice the $\\theta/2$. Advance $\\theta$ by a full turn, $\\theta \\to \\theta + 2\\pi$, and the rotation is unchanged — but $q \\mapsto -q$. Both $q$ and $-q$ give the *same* rotation, since the two sign flips in $q v \\bar q$ cancel.\n\nSo the map $S^3 \\to SO(3)$ is **two-to-one**: the unit quaternions form a sphere that wraps twice around the rotation group. In the language of topology-journey Stage 2.02 this is a double covering, and $S^3$ is the universal cover of $SO(3)$ — which is what \"$SO(3)$ is not simply connected\" means concretely. A rotation by $2\\pi$ is the identity, but the *path* getting there is not contractible; you need $4\\pi$ to come home. The plate trick and the Dirac belt trick are demonstrations of precisely this.\n\nPractically, quaternions have no gimbal lock (no chart degeneracy), compose with 16 multiplications instead of 27, and renormalise stably. This is why they are what flight software and graphics engines actually use.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* Quaternions as [s, v1, v2, v3] */\nqs(q) := q[1]$\nqv(q) := [q[2], q[3], q[4]]$\n\nqmul(p, q) := block([sp, vp, sq, vq, s, v],\n  sp : qs(p), vp : qv(p), sq : qs(q), vq : qv(q),\n  s : sp*sq - vp . vq,\n  v : sp*vq + sq*vp + cross(vp, vq),\n  ratsimp( [s, v[1], v[2], v[3]] ) )$\n\nqconj(q) := [ qs(q), -q[2], -q[3], -q[4] ]$\nqnorm(q) := sqrt( q . q )$\n\n/* Hamilton's relations, recovered from the formula */\none : [1,0,0,0]$  I : [0,1,0,0]$  J : [0,0,1,0]$  K : [0,0,0,1]$\nprint(\"i² = \", qmul(I,I), \"   j² = \", qmul(J,J), \"   k² = \", qmul(K,K))$\nprint(\"ijk = \", qmul(qmul(I,J),K))$\nprint(\"ij = \", qmul(I,J), \"   ji = \", qmul(J,I), \"  — anticommuting\")$\n\n/* Rotation by conjugation */\nqrot(q, v) := qv( qmul( qmul(q, [0, v[1], v[2], v[3]]), qconj(q) ) )$\n\n/* The unit quaternion for axis n, angle θ */\nquat(n, θ) := [ cos(θ/2), sin(θ/2)*n[1], sin(θ/2)*n[2], sin(θ/2)*n[3] ]$\n\n/* Check against Rodrigues on the 120°-about-(1,1,1) example */\nqd : quat(ndiag, 2*π/3)$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"q = \", tsimp(qd), \"   |q| = \", tsimp(qnorm(qd)))$\nprint(\"q e1 q̄ = \", tsimp(qrot(qd, [1,0,0])), \"   (Rodrigues gave (0,1,0))\")$\nprint(\"q e2 q̄ = \", tsimp(qrot(qd, [0,1,0])))$\nprint(\"q e3 q̄ = \", tsimp(qrot(qd, [0,0,1])))$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 8,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "i² =  [-1,0,0,0]    j² =  [-1,0,0,0]    k² =  [-1,0,0,0]\nijk =  [-1,0,0,0]\nij =  [0,0,0,1]    ji =  [0,0,0,-1]   — anticommuting\nq =  [1/2,1/2,1/2,1/2]    |q| =  1\nq e1 q̄ =  [0,1,0]    (Rodrigues gave (0,1,0))\nq e2 q̄ =  [0,0,1]\nq e3 q̄ =  [1,0,0]"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* --- The double cover, made explicit --- */\nqa : quat(nax, θ0)$\nprint(\"q  for (axis, θ)     = \", float(qa))$\nprint(\"q  for (axis, θ+2π)  = \", float(quat(nax, θ0 + 2*π)), \"  — the negative\")$\nprint(\"both send e1 to      : \", float(qrot(qa, [1,0,0])), \" and \",\n                                 float(qrot(-qa, [1,0,0])))$\nprint(\"only θ + 4π returns q itself: \", float(quat(nax, θ0 + 4*π)))$\n\n/* --- Composition is quaternion multiplication --- */\nq1 : quat([1,0,0], π/2)$\nq2 : quat([0,1,0], π/2)$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"q1 q2 applied to e3      = \", tsimp(qrot(qmul(q1, q2), [0,0,1])))$\nprint(\"Rx(90°)Ry(90°) e3        = \",\n      transpose(tsimp( Rx(π/2) . Ry(π/2) . cvec([0,0,1]) )))$\n\n/* --- Quaternion → matrix, checked against Rodrigues symbolically --- */\nqmat(q) := transpose(matrix( qrot(q, [1,0,0]), qrot(q, [0,1,0]), qrot(q, [0,0,1]) ))$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"qmat(quat(n,θ)) − Rodrigues(n,θ), symbolic axis with |n| = 1:\")$\nprint( matrixmap(unit, tsimp( qmat(quat(nsym, θ)) - Rodrigues(nsym, θ) )) )$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 10,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "q  for (axis, θ)     =\n                       [0.9393727128473789,0.0,0.2057386844732708,\n                        0.2743182459643611]\nq  for (axis, θ+2π)  =\n                       [-0.9393727128473789,0.0,-0.2057386844732708,\n                        -0.2743182459643611]   — the negative\nboth send e1 to      :\n                       [0.764842187284489,0.5153741497901525,\n                        -0.3865306123426145]  and\n                       [0.764842187284489,0.5153741497901525,\n                        -0.3865306123426145]\nonly θ + 4π returns q itself:\n                              [0.9393727128473791,0.0,0.20573868447327046,\n                               0.27431824596436066]\nq1 q2 applied to e3      =  [1,0,0]\nRx(90°)Ry(90°) e3        =  matrix([1,0,0])\nqmat(quat(n,θ)) − Rodrigues(n,θ), symbolic axis with |n| = 1:\nmatrix([0,0,0],[0,0,0],[0,0,0])"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "### Measuring the degeneracy\n\nGimbal lock can be quantified rather than merely described. Let the three Euler angles vary with time and compute the angular velocity $\\omega$, defined by $\\widehat\\omega = \\dot R R^{\\mathsf T}$. Because $R$ depends on the angles, $\\omega$ is a linear function of the rates:\n\n$$\\omega = E(\\phi, \\theta, \\psi)\\begin{pmatrix}\\dot\\phi\\\\ \\dot\\theta\\\\ \\dot\\psi\\end{pmatrix}.$$\n\nThe parametrisation is healthy exactly where $E$ is invertible — where every angular velocity is achievable by some choice of rates. Computing $\\det E$ tells us where that fails.\n\n(That $\\dot R R^{\\mathsf T}$ is antisymmetric is itself worth checking: differentiate $RR^{\\mathsf T} = I$ to get $\\dot R R^{\\mathsf T} + R\\dot R^{\\mathsf T} = 0$, which says exactly that $\\dot R R^{\\mathsf T}$ is minus its own transpose. Velocities of curves in $SO(3)$ live in $\\mathfrak{so}(3)$ — the tangent space is the Lie algebra, and this small computation is the reason.)",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* Let the Euler angles depend on time */\ndepends([φ, θ, ψ], t)$\n\nRt : Rz(φ) . Ry(θ) . Rx(ψ)$\nW  : trigsimp( diff(Rt, t) . transpose(Rt) )$\n\nprint(\"Ṙ Rᵀ + (Ṙ Rᵀ)ᵀ = \", trigsimp(W + transpose(W)), \"  — antisymmetric, as claimed\")$\n\n/* ω from the antisymmetric part (vee returns twice the vector) */\nω : trigsimp( vee(W) / 2 )$\n\n/* Extract the matrix E with ω = E · (φ̇, θ̇, ψ̇) */\nrates : [diff(φ,t), diff(θ,t), diff(ψ,t)]$\nE : genmatrix( lambda([i,j], trigsimp(coeff(expand(ω[i]), rates[j]))), 3, 3 )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"E = \", E)$\nprint(\"det E = \", trigsimp(determinant(E)))$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 11,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Ṙ Rᵀ + (Ṙ Rᵀ)ᵀ =  matrix([0,0,0],[0,0,0],[0,0,0])\n                   — antisymmetric, as claimed\nE =\n    matrix([0,-sin(phi),cos(phi)*cos(theta)],[0,cos(phi),sin(phi)*cos(theta)],\n           [1,0,-sin(theta)])\ndet E =  -cos(theta)"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* det E = −cos θ vanishes at θ = ±π/2 — the gimbal-lock configurations.\n   Below, the double cover: the rotation has period 2π, the quaternion 4π. */\n\nplot2d([cos(u), cos(u/2)],\n       [u, 0, 4*π],\n       [legend, \"rotation: tr R = 1 + 2cos θ  (period 2π)\",\n                \"quaternion: scalar part cos(θ/2)  (period 4π)\"],\n       [xlabel, \"θ\"], [ylabel, \"\"],\n       [title, \"one turn returns the rotation; two turns are needed for the quaternion\"])$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 12,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
