   "source": "## Summary\n\n| Object | Description | Cost |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Matrix** $A \\in SO(3)$ | 9 numbers, 6 constraints | composition = 27 multiplications; drifts off $SO(3)$ under rounding |\n| **Axis–angle** $(\\hat n, \\theta)$ | 4 numbers, 1 constraint | geometric meaning is immediate; degenerate at $\\theta = 0, \\pi$ |\n| **Euler angles** $(\\phi,\\theta,\\psi)$ | 3 numbers, no constraints | minimal, but $\\det E = -\\cos\\theta$ vanishes — gimbal lock |\n| **Quaternion** $q \\in S^3$ | 4 numbers, 1 constraint | composition = 16 multiplications; no degeneracy; double-covers $SO(3)$ |\n\nThree results carry forward.\n\n**Euler's theorem** — every rotation of an odd-dimensional space fixes a line. The proof needed $\\det A = 1$ and $n$ odd, and it fails in the plane exactly as it should.\n\n**Every rotation is $\\exp(\\theta K)$ for antisymmetric $K$.** The exponential map takes the flat vector space $\\mathfrak{so}(3)$ onto the curved group $SO(3)$, and $K^3 = -K$ is what makes the series close up into $\\sin$ and $\\cos$. This is the whole shape of Lie theory, visible in $3\\times3$ matrices.\n\n**The unit quaternions double-cover $SO(3)$.** The half-angle in $q = (\\cos\\frac\\theta2, \\sin\\frac\\theta2\\,\\hat n)$ is not a convention — it is the reason $q$ and $-q$ describe the same rotation, and it makes $S^3 \\to SO(3)$ a genuine two-to-one covering map.\n\n## The thread to Stage 5\n\nAlmost every idea here is a special case of something general, and it is worth naming the correspondences now so they are recognisable later:\n\n| Here, for $SO(3)$ | In general (Stage 5) |\n|---|---|\n| antisymmetric matrices $K$ | the Lie algebra $\\mathfrak g$ |\n| $\\hat n \\times v = \\widehat n\\, v$ | the bracket $[\\cdot,\\cdot]$ on $\\mathfrak g$ |\n| $\\exp(\\theta K)$ | the exponential map $\\mathfrak g \\to G$ |\n| $\\dot R R^{\\mathsf T}$ antisymmetric | tangent vectors to $G$ live in $\\mathfrak g$ |\n| $S^3 \\to SO(3)$ two-to-one | $SU(2) \\to SO(3)$, the universal cover |\n| gimbal lock | no global chart on a non-trivial manifold |\n\n## Next\n\n**1.04 — Symmetry Groups** stops looking at all of $SO(3)$ and starts looking at its *finite* subgroups. The answer is a short and famous list — cyclic, dihedral, and the symmetry groups of the tetrahedron, cube and dodecahedron — and it is the reason there are exactly five Platonic solids.",
   "metadata": {}
